<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Christopher Marlowe</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1564">1564</a>–<a href="/years/1593">1593</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Christopher Marlowe was an English playwright and poet of the Elizabethan age whose plays Tamburlaine, Doctor Faustus, The Jew of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malta">Malta</a> and Edward II established blank verse as the main form of English drama. Educated at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a>, he was linked to government secret service and was killed in a quarrel at Deptford in 1593, aged 29. His work strongly influenced the young Shakespeare.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/christopher-marlowe.jpg" alt="Christopher Marlowe" width="320" height="404" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: anonymous · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Christopher_Marlowe.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Christopher Marlowe was the second of nine children of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canterbury">Canterbury</a> shoemaker John Marlowe and his wife Katherine, daughter of William Arthur of Dover. He was baptised at St George's Church, Canterbury, on 26 February 1564, and was probably born a few days earlier, about two months before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-shakespeare">William Shakespeare</a>. Little is known of his adult life beyond legal records. In 1587 the Privy Council ordered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a> to award him his Master of Arts, denying rumours that he meant to go to the English Catholic college at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reims">Rheims</a> and saying he had been engaged in matters touching the benefit of his country. Some writers conclude that he was a government spy, though that is speculation.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Six dramas have been attributed to Marlowe, alone or in collaboration: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dido">Dido</a>, Queen of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a>, Tamburlaine, The Jew of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malta">Malta</a>, Doctor Faustus, Edward the Second and The Massacre at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>. Tamburlaine, first performed on a regular <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> stage in 1587, was the first English play in blank verse and, with Thomas Kyd's The Spanish Tragedy, is seen as the start of the mature phase of Elizabethan theatre. His plays formed the foundation of the repertoire of the Admiral's Men, and the haughty roles of Tamburlaine, Faustus and Barabas were probably written for the tall actor Edward Alleyn. Doctor Faustus is the first dramatised version of the Faust legend, and its hero is carried off by demons.</p><p>His poetry includes The Passionate Shepherd to His Love and the unfinished <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hero-and-leander">Hero and Leander</a>, completed by George Chapman and printed in 1598, as well as translations of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ovid">Ovid</a>'s Amores and of the first book of Lucan's Pharsalia. In 1599 the Ovid translation was banned and copies were publicly burned.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In May 1593 bills threatening Protestant refugees were posted in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, one signed Tamburlaine. The next day Thomas Kyd was arrested, and a heretical tract found in his lodgings was said by Kyd, under torture, to be Marlowe's. A warrant for Marlowe's arrest was issued on 18 May, and he presented himself on 20 May. He was killed on 30 May 1593, and scholars still dispute the circumstances. George Peele called him the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muses">Muses</a>' darling, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ben-jonson">Ben Jonson</a> wrote of Marlowe's mighty line.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Christopher Marlowe” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Elizabethan and Jacobean age produced the drama of William Shakespeare, <b>Christopher Marlowe</b> and Ben Jonson, and the King James Bible of 1611 shaped English prose for centuries.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-england">Kingdom of England · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canterbury">Canterbury</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in Deptford, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.28,1.08&amp;year=1582" aria-label="Canterbury on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4574 387 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Christopher Marlowe"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5024 657h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5024 657h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.28,1.08&amp;year=1582">What was here in 1582? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Was this the face that launch'd a thousand ships, And burnt the topless towers of Ilium?— Sweet Helen, make me immortal with a kiss.&#8221;</p><footer>Doctor Faustus, act 5 &#183; original &#183; spoken by Faustus to the spirit of Helen of Troy &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/811/pg811-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Hell hath no limits, nor is circumscrib'd In one self-place; but where we are is hell, And where hell is, there must we ever be…&#8221;</p><footer>Doctor Faustus, act 2 &#183; original &#183; spoken by the devil Mephistophilis &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/811/pg811-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;The stars move still, time runs, the clock will strike, The devil will come, and Faustus must be damn'd.&#8221;</p><footer>Doctor Faustus, act 5 (the last hour) &#183; original &#183; spoken by Faustus as midnight nears &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/811/pg811-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>2 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Why, this is hell, nor am I out of it: Think'st thou that I, that saw the face of God, And tasted the eternal joys of heaven, Am not tormented with ten thousand hells, In being depriv'd of everlasting bliss?&#8221;</p><footer>Doctor Faustus, act 1 &#183; original &#183; spoken by Mephistophilis to Faustus &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/811/pg811-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Doth teach us all to have aspiring minds: Our souls, whose faculties can comprehend The wondrous architecture of the world, And measure every wandering planet's course, Still climbing after knowledge infinite, And always moving as the restless spheres…&#8221;</p><footer>Tamburlaine the Great, part 1 (c. 1587), act 2 &#183; original &#183; spoken by Tamburlaine &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/1094/pg1094-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 540 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
